hi everyone i'm jennifer zabasaja of  bloomberg news thank you  so much for joining us so this is a  really important conversation that we  are about to have even before joe biden  named senator kamala harris as his vice  presidential running mate  several women were preparing for the  inevitable  how the media how everyone really would  would have a double standard of female  candidates  versus male candidates and it's part of  the reason why i asked these two guests  to come  today to speak with me a little bit  about this so joining me today is  time's up ceo and president tina chen  and also editor at large of the 19th  aaron haynes how's it going both of you  it's going great happy black women's  equal pay day  august that's the point it takes this  long for black women  to earn what white men make yeah it's  yes it's something that i do want to get  into with both of you but aaron i wanted  to start with you first  even before senator harris was named as  the vice presidential pick you spoke  with people who said  a black candidate was a requirement not  a recommendation  can you just tell us in your opinion  what does she bring to this ticket  yeah well i mean obviously this is a  historic pick with her being uh the  first black woman the first woman of  south asian indian descent  being on this ticket uh but really what  she brings is a combination of her  qualifications and lived experience  uh to the ticket you heard uh her  alluding to some of that  yesterday giving a nod to the heroic and  ambitious women that had come  before her uh you know but this is  somebody who you know has already been  you know a trailblazer in in her  political career and and as we sit here  today  is the highest uh ranking black uh  woman elected official in the country  and so  you know i think that we have seen uh  her also making history with lone black  woman on the campaign trail in the 2020  democratic presidential  primary and you know in this pandemic uh  has been somebody who has  centered marginalized communities  raising issues about you know  unemployment benefits  uh eviction uh moratoriums uh  asking for uh you know racial data uh in  terms of this  global public health crisis and how  black folks who we know are being  disproportionately affected  uh by the coronavirus uh you know having  that  in writing and so that those resources  can be appropriately  uh distributed to those communities and  so i think those were really  among the criteria that joe biden was  considering  uh you know when he was trying to choose  from among the very uh  qualified capable and talented women uh  who were on  that list yeah and and tina i want to  ask you what was your reaction to  seeing the two of them for the first  time yesterday well it was thrilling  you know i i i would say this is a  historic moment for our country  even regardless of your party  affiliation you know this  this is real progress and just seeing a  black and  api woman standing up there you know  along with you know an incredible man as  a leader  vice president biden who's going to lead  us out of this pandemic  crisis economic crisis racial justice  reckoning that this country is going  through right now we need  these two leaders who can combine their  talents and address all of those issues  but mark my words we know what's coming  for her and  as we are saying it times up we have her  back because we  know what is coming and it has already  happened you know it just  you know it doesn't take even an hour  for the  racial misogynistic attacks to come her  way  and we're not going to stand for it as  women leaders in this country  yeah and your group at time's up was  very immediate to come out with we have  her back tell us a little bit about  how it came about and what exactly  you're planning on doing throughout this  election season  well for the last oh i apologize we have  for the last couple of couple weeks you  know we have many women leaders have  been talking  because we've lived this movie before we  have been here before we've seen what if  what has happened  to women leaders um and they've been  attacked and we weren't going to stand  for it let it go by unanswered this time  because when it happens you know when  women are called  nasty or their ambition is you're denied  or they talk about what they wear you  know we don't see women as leaders then  and it's not just as leaves our country  it's leaders you know as a ceo of a  company it's the  restaurant manager the server who wants  to become a restaurant manager  doesn't get seen in that leadership  position um so this affects our entire  culture  so we aren't going to stanford anymore  we're going to stand up and do it we  issued a memo to editorial rooms  last thursday night it didn't take 24  hours last weekend  for the new york times to run a headline  about wrist corsages  and the vice presidential you know  selection and the la times to liken  this to the bachelor and then you now  see  in less than 24 hours the attacks coming  at kamala harris  the other thing that happened in the  last 24 hours was the speaker of the  house  being called the b word and the gop  leaders letting that go by  this is all about demeaning and trying  to bring down women leaders so that we  aren't able to ascend to leadership and  a lot of the misinformation  is really spread through social media  right and then it just gets picked up  and  amplified aaron i wonder if someone who  works in a newsroom  what are ways that you know people can  you know  fairly criticize some of these female  candidates without actually adding to  a lot of the noise that is out there  yeah well you know aside from starting a  whole new newsroom which is what we did  uh you know this year tina we got the  memo uh back in january when we launched  the 19th uh you know because we had seen  uh frankly uh that gendered coverage not  only in the 2016  election but in presidential cycle after  cycle uh  you know just really focusing on you  know personality traits questions of  electability  as if you know the definition of  electability is is not getting elected  right  uh and so you know really yes i mean i  think that that's it  focusing on on qualifications and really  just taking a a moment to think  uh you know before uh asking uh women  candidates whether they're running for  president or anywhere uh up or down the  ticket  uh is this a question that i would ask a  man uh that  was running for office and if the answer  is no then you know why is that question  being  uh posed uh to the woman that is running  for that office and so i think that  that's a starting point uh but i also  think that frankly part of the problem  uh which you know we are  certainly attempting to be a solution  for at the 19th  is the reality that three-fourths of the  stories that are written  and the gatekeepers who are are doing  political journalism are  white and or male and or both uh and so  when you have that  uh it it it is at least part of the  explanation for  uh why these narratives persist uh but  you are seeing so much pushback both  inside and outside  of newsrooms uh to to really kind of uh  make sure that these kinds of tropes and  these kinds of narratives  uh have no place uh in 2020 election and  beyond in our political journalism  yeah and it's important to tina i think  to mention we have her back is  bipartisan right it's not just about  carmel harris and the democrats you also  have people on the republican side who  are supporting this right  oh absolutely i mean this let's be clear  you know gender discrimination  and misogyny knows no partisan line  it happens in you know all across the  spectrum and when it happens we're going  to call it out  because voters need to understand what  they're seeing you know  some of these some of this language is  so coded they don't even realize it  you know when president trump calls  kamal harris phony  you know it sounds like that's not a  sexist word but guess what  it actually plays into the idea that  she's a phony because women don't belong  running for leadership right it it is  sending that message  and it's sending that message not just  to kamala harris it's sending it to  our daughters and to our sons about how  they see women in leadership  and that's why culture change you know  deep culture change requires us  to call this out so the voters see it  and understand it and are informed when  they take make their vote  this november and that beyond november  we begin to see women leadership for  what it is which is we deserve to be  there we deserve to be in the white  house  we just deserve to be at the top of  these companies  and our country is going to be better  for it  and finally just to both of you do you  worry i mean we're living in this age  where cancer culture right is is  such a huge thing do you worry that  you know people could this could lead to  maybe people trying to cancel  out certain people and and fueling that  even more or  um i wonder what your take is on that  well you know  at least at times up and up we have our  back we're focused on what words are  being used  right and change your language and  change your viewpoint  you know don't don't we we want you you  know to news editors  you know to actually think more and use  different language  not exit the stage um and we want people  to be  thoughtful so we're focused on the words  on what's being used and what's being  said  and do better and we're not saying don't  criticize folks you know as aaron said  right  talk about the issues talk about her  record right and if you disagree with  those  have at it but don't talk about her  clothes don't talk about her personal  past  don't talk about you know her husband um  and  don't you know even if you think you're  cute don't make allusions to the  bachelor  do not do that yeah and i would just say  i mean look  at the 19th we're we're about cancelling  uh the myth of electability and and and  you know questions of likability and  anything that is not you know  focused on uh what what this should be  about which is  uh the news and information that voters  are going to need to make an informed uh  decision about who they're going to vote  for uh in in  less than 90 days from now absolutely  and aaron just finally you're speaking  with senator harris tomorrow  is there anything that you can share  with us uh what can we expect  well i think that you can expect a  robust conversation uh we  expect that uh our uh conversation with  senator harris which is going to be our  final keynote at the end of our virtual  summit  uh that has already been spectacular and  has been been going on this entire week  to to really recognize  uh the upcoming uh centennial of the  19th amendment for which our newsroom is  named but obviously with an asterisk  for the omission of the black women who  were excluded uh from  access to that franchise for another  four decades until the passage of the  voting rights act  of 1965 and so uh you know we're going  to have an extended conversation  with senator harris around a range of  issues including you know why  uh she uh you know the case that she  made for for why she uh was was  a fit for this role uh what she plans to  bring to this ticket  uh you know and frankly those questions  of race and and gender and  her response to them among other things  so i hope that everybody will tune in  it's going to be uh tomorrow at  2 p.m eastern and you can register for  free at summit.19news.org so i hope that  everybody will join us  and i love that you're doing this erin i  love i love the 19th and i  love love that senator harris is going  to be at your first ever summit  congratulations so much tina we're super  excited  that the vice presidential uh nominee a  woman on the ballot for the third time  uh in vice presidential  uh politics for a major party and the  first time that a woman of color is  joining that ticket so uh yes it is very  exciting indeed that she will be  joining our new but mighty uh newsroom  uh to discuss these issues tomorrow  amazing well thank you both so much for  this conversation  please come back because we have 81 or  something day so i think we're going to  have a lot more to talk about  yeah uh editor at large of the 19th  aaron haines and  time's up ceo and president tina chen  thank you both for your time  thank you take care everybody

